<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">H. G. Wells</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Herbert George Wells</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herbert George <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> was an English writer, born in Bromley, who wrote more than forty novels and dozens of short stories besides works of social commentary and history. A student of biology under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley">Thomas Henry Huxley</a>, he made his name with science fiction: The Time Machine (1895), The Island of Doctor Moreau (1896), The Invisible Man (1897) and The War of the Worlds (1898) imagined time travel, alien invasion and invisibility before these were common in the genre. Novels such as Kipps (1905) and Tono-Bungay (1909) described English society. An outspoken socialist, he was for much of his adult life an advocate of eugenics, a view he had largely recanted by 1940. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/h-g-wells.jpg" alt="H. G. Wells" width="330" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Charles Beresford · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H.G._Wells_by_Beresford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>CBS aired Orson Welles's radio adaptation of <b>H. G. Wells</b>'s novel as a series of mock news bulletins about a Martian landing in New Jersey.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
